@page "/api"
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@inject IOptions<PortalOptions> Options
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "API and feeds";
    var site = Options.Value.PublicUrl.Length > 0 ? Options.Value.PublicUrl.TrimEnd('/') : $"{Request.Scheme}://{Request.Host}";
}
<h1>API and feeds</h1>
<p class="lead">For tools, agents and feed readers. Read-only, no key, JSON or Atom. Channels themselves are plain files: Pkg reads them directly.</p>

<h2 class="label">Search</h2>
<pre><code>curl '@site/api/search?q=rexx'</code></pre>
<p>The results of the search page, as JSON. Parameters, all optional: <code>q</code>, <code>channel</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>arch</code>, <code>tag</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>sort</code> (<code>newest</code> or <code>downloads</code>; relevance otherwise), <code>take</code> (1 to 500, 100 by default). Each result gives the channel, name, latest version, kind, CPUs, short description, category, tags, the libraries it provides, authors, licence, signer, last update, downloads, why it matched when not by name, its page, its channel address and the command that installs it.</p>

<h2 class="label">One package</h2>
<pre><code>curl '@site/api/packages/pkg/pkg'</code></pre>
<p>The same fields, plus every version with its CPU, the date the portal first published it, the manifest and payload digests, the signer, downloads, and whether its publisher withdrew it.</p>

<h2 class="label">This portal's rules</h2>
<pre><code>curl '@site/api/policy'</code></pre>
<p>What this instance accepts: uploads at all, binaries or links only, which archive hosts, whether a push may create a channel, plain http. A refusal caused by one of these rules names it (<code>policy: Binaries=off</code>) with the operators' note.</p>

<h2 class="label">Feeds</h2>
<p>Atom, the 50 newest versions first: <a href="/feed">every channel</a>, or one channel at <code>/channels/&lt;name&gt;/feed</code>, such as <a href="/channels/contrib-nightly/feed">contrib-nightly</a>.</p>

<h2 class="label">Badges</h2>
<p>One address per package, <code>/b/&lt;name&gt;</code>: it answers with the badge to a README and with the package's page to a person who clicks it, right at the command that installs it. Nothing else to write down.</p>
<p><img src="/b/pkg" alt="pkg badge" /></p>
@{ var snippet = $"[![pkg]({site}/b/pkg)]({site}/b/pkg)"; }
<div class="cmd wrapcode"><code>@snippet</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@snippet">Copy</button></div>
<p class="muted small">Two channels carrying the same name: <code>/b/&lt;channel&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code> says which. <code>/badge/&lt;name&gt;.svg</code> and <code>/badge/&lt;channel&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.svg</code> are the badge alone, for anything that insists on an address ending in <code>.svg</code>.</p>

<h2 class="label">How Pkg spreads</h2>
<p><a href="/api/usage">/api/usage</a> hands over every tally the portal keeps: for each day, how many channel reads, packages taken and pushes came from each Pkg version, system and CPU. That is all of it; there is nothing per request, per address or per person. <a href="/privacy">Privacy</a> says what is kept and what is not.</p>

<h2 class="label">Downloads counted</h2>
<p>A signed package the portal serves, fetched from its first byte, counts once; so does each way of getting Pkg. Archives kept by their makers, such as the nightly contrib build, are not seen by the portal and not counted.</p>
